course: dictionary
lesson: 470
title: Array: things in neat rows
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An array is a neat set of things arranged in rows and columns. You can often work out its meaning from the words around it: «When things stand in rows and every row is the same length, they make an array.» The clue is "rows" and "same length", so an array is things lined up neatly.
explain: Think of an egg carton. It has 2 rows of 6 eggs, so there are 12 eggs in all. You can count it as 2 × 6 or as 6 × 2, and you get the same total either way.
explain: The word also has a bigger, looser meaning. «An array of colors» means a large, mixed group of different things set out to be seen. In computing, an array is a list of values kept in order. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
fact: An array is a set of things in equal rows and columns.
fact: To find the total of an array, multiply the rows by the columns: 3 × 4 = 12.
fact: Swapping the rows and columns gives the same total: 3 × 4 = 4 × 3.
fact: Everyday arrays include egg cartons, chocolate bars and window panes.
fact: «An array of colors» means a large, mixed group of things; in computing, an array is a list of values in order.
exercise: «When things stand in rows and every row is the same length, they make an array.» What must be true for things to make an array? | They are scattered on the floor | Every thing is a different colour | *Every row is the same length | There is only one thing
exercise: An egg carton has 2 rows of 6 eggs. How many eggs are there in total? | 26 | 8 | *12 | 3
exercise: «An array of colors» fills the art room wall. In this sentence, what does array mean? | An empty box | A single colour | A quiet sound | *A large, mixed group of things
test: Why does 3 × 4 give the same total as 4 × 3 in an array? | Because 3 and 4 are odd numbers | *Because you can swap the rows and columns and the total stays the same | Because the rows are always longer than the columns | Because multiplication always gives a bigger answer
test: Which everyday thing is an example of an array? | A cloud in the sky | A ball of string | A puddle on the path | *A chocolate bar with its squares in rows
exam: A box holds 5 rows with 4 chocolates in each row. How many chocolates are there in total? | 45 | 54 | 9 | *20
